    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Each army had about 40,000 men; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> were stronger in cavalry and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in infantry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> chose a flat flood plain where his cavalry could be used to greatest effect.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> used his Numidian cavalry to draw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> out before breakfast and across the chest-high, icy river, while 2,000 men under his brother Mago lay hidden in an old watercourse.</li><li>After the battle Gallic recruits flocked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and his army grew to 60,000, and in spring 217 BCE he moved south into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sempronius sent his army across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> in three columns, each 4 kilometres long, through water running chest-high on a cold, snowy morning, and few of his men are thought to have eaten breakfast.</p></aside>
    </section>

