    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">219 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> besieges, captures and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> declares war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and enters what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Carthaginian cavalry beats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the Ticinus, and Publius Scipio is wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Sempronius brings his army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to join Scipio on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> draws the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> and defeats them (on 22 or 23 December)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late 218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> are recognised as the dominant force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> and their army grows to 60,000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> marches to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and wins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-trebia" data-v="2e030438e5" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Numidians provoke the Romans</b> Hannibal sent his Numidian cavalry across the river to force back the Roman pickets and provoke a fight. Mago had already taken 1,000 cavalry and 1,000 infantry in the night to an old watercourse, where they hid.</li><li><b>The Romans ford the Trebia after the Numidians</b> Sempronius sent out his cavalry, then the velites and then the whole army. The Numidians drew back slowly, and the Romans followed them through the icy, chest-high water of the Trebia.</li><li><b>The armies form up on the west bank</b> The Romans organised themselves in battle formation west of the Trebia, facing the Carthaginian army, which had formed up symmetrically. The light infantry of both sides stood between the lines. Mago's force lay hidden in a watercourse to the south.</li><li><b>The Carthaginian skirmishers drive back the velites</b> The velites had used many javelins against the cavalry and were unfed, tired and cold, while the Carthaginian slingers outranged them. The Carthaginians got the better of the skirmishing and drove the velites back through the gaps in their heavy infantry.</li><li><b>The skirmishers harass the Roman cavalry</b> The Carthaginian light infantry moved towards the flanks of their army and harassed the Roman cavalry with their missiles. The Roman horses were already tired from chasing the Numidians.</li><li><b>The Roman cavalry flees and the infantry lines close</b> On each wing 5,000 Carthaginian horse charged the 2,000 Roman horse, which fled back over the Trebia. Most of the Carthaginian cavalry pursued them while the heavy infantry lines met in the centre. The skirmishers fell back behind their cavalry.</li><li><b>Carthaginian skirmishers go round the exposed flank</b> The Carthaginian light infantry moved forward and round the Roman flank, which the flight of the cavalry had left open. The velites turned to face them and many allied infantry on the flanks turned too, which slowed their push on the front.</li><li><b>Mago's force strikes the Roman left rear</b> Mago's men came down the watercourse unnoticed and charged the velites, who were already holding off the Carthaginian light infantry. The velites held their formation.</li><li><b>The Carthaginian cavalry returns to the Roman rear</b> Increasing numbers of Carthaginian cavalry broke off the pursuit, came back and attacked the Roman rear. Under this strain the allied infantry on the flanks and the velites began to break up.</li><li><b>The Roman centre breaks through the Gauls</b> The Roman infantry in the centre routed the 8,000 Gauls facing them and broke through the middle of the Carthaginian army. Behind them the rest of the Roman army had dissolved.</li><li><b>Ten thousand Romans reach Placentia</b> Sempronius ordered the infantry away from the field. Keeping its formation, 10,000 of them recrossed the Trebia and reached Placentia. The Carthaginians cut down the partly surrounded rest of the Roman army.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca.jpg" alt="Hamilcar Barca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amilcare_Barca_(Ritratto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Max Karl Baldamus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, fought from 264 to 241 BCE, ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> defeated after 23 years. Five years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthaginian-iberia">Carthaginian Iberia</a> and made it a quasi-monarchical, autonomous territory ruled by his family, the Barcids. It gave Carthage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, agricultural wealth, manpower and shipyards, which encouraged it to resist future Roman demands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> succeeded his father-in-law Hasdrubal in 221 BCE, and in 219 BCE his army besieged, captured and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, a city with a treaty of association with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Rome declared war on Carthage.</p><p>In 218 BCE Rome raised an army for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> under the consul Publius Scipio. The Gallic tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, antagonised by Roman colonists settled at Placentia and Cremona on Gallic land, rose and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The Senate took a Roman and an allied legion from the Iberian force to send to the region, so the Scipios could not set out until September. Hannibal marched from Iberia in May, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in October, and came down into what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> with 20,000 infantry, 6,000 cavalry and about 30 elephants. He needed food and Gallic allies, so he stormed the capital of the unwelcoming Taurini and massacred its population as a message to the other tribes.</p><p>Publius Scipio, back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, led the cavalry and light infantry of his army against the Carthaginians at the Ticinus, where the Romans were soundly beaten and Scipio was wounded. The Romans retreated to Placentia, and then to high ground by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> at what is now Rivergaro, to await reinforcement.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Trebia&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-war, War in Cisalpine Gaul, Carthage invades Italy, First contact:</p><ul><li>John Lazenby, Hannibal's War: A Military History of the Second Punic War (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1998)</li><li>Dexter Hoyos, Mastering the West: Rome and Carthage at War (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li><li>John Lazenby, The First Punic War: A Military History (Stanford University Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The consul Sempronius Longus had been assembling an army in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> the next year. The Senate ordered it north, and it reached Ariminum (modern Rimini) after 40 days, probably part of the way by sea. It joined Scipio's army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, and because Scipio was still partly incapacitated by his wounds Sempronius took overall command.</p><p>The Roman force had four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a>, which at full strength should have mustered 16,800 men including 4,800 velites, though at least one was significantly understrength. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> gives 16,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, writing 200 years later, 18,000. There were also about 20,000 allied infantry, four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> legions and a strong force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and 4,000 cavalry of Romans, Latins and Gauls. Mention is made of 6,000 light infantry, which most modern historians take to be included in the 36,000 or 38,000 infantry.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> had 29,000 infantry, 21,000 of them close-order and 8,000 light, and 11,000 cavalry, a mixture of Africans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> and Gauls; 8,000 of the close-order infantry were Gauls, and the other proportions are not known. He also had about 30 elephants, survivors of the 37 with which he had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> recruited foreigners, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, so the army's strength was in cavalry and skirmishers, and he preferred to fight on the flat, open flood plain, where his cavalry could manoeuvre. Sempronius wanted a battle before Scipio recovered and before new consuls took office in less than three months.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the night before, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> sent his younger brother Mago with 1,000 cavalry and 1,000 infantry to hide in an old watercourse full of brush south of the field. The morning of 22 or 23 December was cold and snowy. Before daybreak Hannibal sent Numidian cavalry across the river to push back the Roman pickets and provoke a fight, while the rest of his army ate an early breakfast. Sempronius sent out all his cavalry, then his 6,000 velites, and finally his whole army, in three columns each 4 kilometres long, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, which was running chest-high. Few, if any, of his men had eaten.</p><p>The Carthaginian army formed symmetrically: 8,000 Gallic infantry in the centre, 6,000 African and Iberian veterans on each side, half of the elephants beyond each of these and 5,000 cavalry on each wing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> put their heavy infantry in the centre, perhaps 13,000 strong, with about 17,000 allies on the flanks and cavalry on the wings. The Carthaginian light infantry, fully supplied and with slingers who outranged the velites, drove the cold, hungry velites back.</p><p>On each wing 5,000 Carthaginian cavalry met 2,000 Roman, and both contests were quickly decided, the Romans fleeing back over the river. The Carthaginian light infantry then moved round the exposed Roman flanks, and the Roman allied infantry turned to face them. Mago's 2,000 came down the watercourse into the Roman left rear. Part of the Roman line broke, but the Roman centre routed the 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and an African unit and broke through, only to find the rest of the army dissolved. Sempronius led 10,000 back across the river to Placentia.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Trebia&#8221; cites where it covers Early stages, Formations, Engagement:</p><ul><li>John Lazenby, The First Punic War: A Military History (Stanford University Press, 1996)</li><li>Brian Todd Carey, Hannibal's Last Battle: Zama &amp; the Fall of Carthage (Pen &amp; Sword, 2007)</li><li>Archer Jones, The Art of War in the Western World (University of Illinois Press, 1987)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Modern historians debate the Roman losses. Dexter Hoyos states that the only survivors were the infantry who broke through the Carthaginian centre, and Richard Miles says that many of those outside this group were killed. Goldsworthy says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> suffered heavily but that numbers of soldiers straggled into Placentia or the camps. John Lazenby argues that, outside a group of 12,500, few infantry escaped but most of the cavalry did. According to Paul Erdkamp the Romans lost 20,000 killed, half of their force, not counting the captured. Carthaginian losses are generally agreed to have been several thousand of the Gallic infantry in the centre, fewer of their other infantry and cavalry, and several elephants. Many African infantry were re-equipped with captured Roman armour and weapons.</p><p>The wounded Scipio gathered the guards left in the camps and marched to Placentia. News of the defeat caused panic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which calmed once Sempronius arrived to preside over the consular elections. Gnaeus Servilius Geminus and Gaius Flaminius were chosen, and the Carthaginian cavalry isolated Placentia and Cremona, which could be supplied by boat up the Po. The consuls-elect raised further legions, reinforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, garrisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>, built a fleet of 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a> and set up supply depots at Ariminum and Arretium. Two armies of four legions each were formed, one at Arretium and one on the Adriatic coast.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Trebia&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>John Lazenby, The First Punic War: A Military History (Stanford University Press, 1996)</li><li>Dexter Hoyos, Hannibal's Dynasty: Power and Politics in the Western Mediterranean, 247–183 BC (Routledge, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a near-contemporary Greek historian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, who wrote about 200 years later, are the sources the modern accounts rest on, and they differ. Polybius gives the Roman force as 16,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Livy as 18,000. Polybius holds that after the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> were recognised as the dominant force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> and that most Gallic tribes sent supplies and recruits, so that the army grew to 60,000. Livy claims the Carthaginians suffered from a shortage of food throughout the winter and gives dramatic accounts of winter confrontations, which Goldsworthy describes as probably an invention.</p><p>Much is uncertain. The date is 22 or 23 December. The sources are unclear whether the 6,000 light infantry are part of the infantry totals, how many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> fought with the Romans and where they stood, and whether the elephants carried towers. Roman losses are debated, from the view that the only survivors were the formed group that broke through to the view that many stragglers and most of the cavalry also escaped, and the size of that group is put at 10,000 or 12,500.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene.jpg" alt="Battle of Lake Trasimene" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene">Battle of Lake Trasimene</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylvestre_Ducar_decapite_Flaminius_(Trasimene).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph-Noël Sylvestre, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> was the first major battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> and a heavy defeat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> as the dominant force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, and Gallic recruits flocked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. The Roman army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which had been meant for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, had been drawn north and beaten, and the Senate's response was to raise new legions and a fleet and to guard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, Sicily and the south.</p><p>In spring 217 BCE, probably early May, Hannibal crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> unopposed by a difficult but unguarded route. He cut the supply line of the consul Gaius Flaminius to Rome, provoked him into a hasty pursuit and set an ambush, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene">Battle of Lake Trasimene</a> he surprised and completely defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, killing Flaminius and another 15,000 Romans and taking 15,000 prisoner.</p><p>In 216 BCE Hannibal marched to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a>, which Richard Miles describes as Rome's greatest military disaster. Toni Ñaco del Hoyo describes the Trebia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and Cannae as the three great military calamities suffered by the Romans in the first three years of the war. The Carthaginians campaigned in southern Italy for a further 13 years. In 204 BCE the younger Publius Cornelius Scipio, the son of the Scipio wounded at the Ticinus, invaded the Carthaginian homeland, and Hannibal was recalled and defeated at Zama in October 202 BCE, after which Carthage agreed a peace that stripped it of most of its territory and power.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> in December 218 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> drew Sempronius's army across an icy river and, with cavalry, light infantry and a hidden force, defeated it heavily; 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> reached Placentia. Gallic recruits joined Hannibal, and the war moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>.</em></p>

